From ccf923edefb5097c4ed067e6667933f03e9d632b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reinette Chatre <reinette.chatre@intel.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Oct 2024 10:48:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 164/164] KVM: selftests: Handle individual test results

KVM TDX selftests rely on TEST_ASSERT() that will run exit() if the test
assertion fails. In some cases a test program contains several individual
tests, each with a TEST_ASSERT() that may prevent the other tests in the
program from running.

commit

        713ab7b0572c ("KVM: selftests: TDX: Add TDX lifecycle test")

introduced run_in_new_process() to address this issue by running each test
in a new process context, preventing an individual TEST_ASSERT() from
killing the main test program.

run_in_new_process() does not pass the test result to the main program,
resulting in all tests run by program to always be considered as passing
no matter how many of them failed their TEST_ASSERT().

Let run_in_new_process() return failure if the test run by it failed its
TEST_ASSERT(). Rely on the kselftest framework to track how many tests are
run and accurately report individual test success.

One unexpected change may be the removal of the "setbuf(stdout, NULL)"
used to disable stdout buffering. Needed stdout buffering changes
are now done by kselftest framework within ksft_print_header() that
after

commit
        58e2847ad2e6 ("selftests: line buffer test program's stdout")

ensured that stdout is flushed before any fork() to prevent duplicate
messages to stdout.

Signed-off-by: Reinette Chatre <reinette.chatre@intel.com>
---
